WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:05.353
Type a sentence, and today's models can animate a whole body.

2
00:00:05.403 --> 00:00:10.255
But look closely at the hands, the way we grip, type, play, and gesture,

3
00:00:10.305 --> 00:00:13.728
and you'll see that progress has lagged far behind.

4
00:00:13.778 --> 00:00:15.157
The reason is simple:

5
00:00:15.207 --> 00:00:17.200
there hasn't been enough data.

6
00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:19.780
Meet RoMo Hands:

7
00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:25.240
a large-scale, richly organized dataset for turning text into hand motion.

8
00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:30.102
Body-motion datasets run to a thousand hours and more,

9
00:00:30.152 --> 00:00:32.543
but treat the hands as rigid blocks.

10
00:00:32.593 --> 00:00:37.155
Studio capture gets every finger right, but covers only a few hours,

11
00:00:37.205 --> 00:00:38.986
and rarely comes with text.

12
00:00:39.036 --> 00:00:43.597
And the newest text-paired hand datasets top out at around 80 hours.

13
00:00:43.647 --> 00:00:46.920
Hands need body-scale data, paired with language.

14
00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:51.900
RoMo Hands has over 540,000 two-handed sequences.

15
00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:55.217
That's 884 hours, 95 million frames, and 2.

16
00:00:55.267 --> 00:00:56.683
7 million captions.

17
00:00:56.733 --> 00:01:01.929
It's more than ten times longer than the largest prior hand dataset,

18
00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:07.560
putting hand motion in the same league as body motion for the first time.

19
00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:12.276
To build it, we start from in-the-wild web video.

20
00:01:12.326 --> 00:01:15.792
The RoMo pipeline recovers each person's 3D body.

21
00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:21.316
Then we estimate both hands with WiLoR, and anchor them to the body's wrists,

22
00:01:21.366 --> 00:01:24.473
so every hand moves in a stable world frame.

23
00:01:24.523 --> 00:01:26.554
Next come two strict filters.

24
00:01:26.604 --> 00:01:33.801
One drops clips where the hands are hard to see; the other drops clips where the fingers barely move.

25
00:01:33.851 --> 00:01:36.169
Of about 1,200 hours, 884 remain:

26
00:01:36.219 --> 00:01:38.680
real, dexterous, two-handed motion.

27
00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:44.634
Every clip is described at five levels of detail, from a two-word tag,

28
00:01:44.684 --> 00:01:50.068
like "tying shoelaces", up to a full paragraph on finger curl, palm orientation,

29
00:01:50.118 --> 00:01:51.358
timing, and intent.

30
00:01:51.408 --> 00:01:56.520
Models can learn from short tags, rich descriptions, or anything in between.

31
00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:01.837
Every clip also sits in a three-level action taxonomy:

32
00:02:01.887 --> 00:02:05.039
category, sub-category, and atomic action.

33
00:02:05.089 --> 00:02:08.698
Cooking, to stovetop cooking, to stirring a pot.

34
00:02:08.748 --> 00:02:11.824
Shopping, to checkout, to handling money.

35
00:02:11.874 --> 00:02:15.025
Gestures, to illustrating with both hands.

36
00:02:15.075 --> 00:02:17.465
And repair, to removing a screw.

37
00:02:17.515 --> 00:02:23.640
In total, 54 categories and over 2,000 sub-categories, all centered on the hands.

38
00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:27.099
Compared with existing hand datasets,

39
00:02:27.149 --> 00:02:31.739
RoMo Hands also covers a much wider range of wrist trajectories, hand poses,

40
00:02:31.789 --> 00:02:34.120
and the language used to describe them.

41
00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:38.394
To plug hands into modern motion generators,

42
00:02:38.444 --> 00:02:42.236
we introduce a 393-dimensional bimanual representation.

43
00:02:42.286 --> 00:02:45.589
It puts a synthetic root between the two wrists,

44
00:02:45.639 --> 00:02:51.177
aligns the heading with the line joining them, and stacks positions, velocities,

45
00:02:51.227 --> 00:02:53.413
and rotations for all 32 joints.

46
00:02:53.463 --> 00:02:54.251
With MoMask,

47
00:02:54.301 --> 00:02:59.560
it lifts top-one retrieval from about 40 percent with raw MANO to nearly 55.

48
00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:05.774
We then benchmark diffusion, masked-token, and autoregressive generators,

49
00:03:05.824 --> 00:03:07.354
and the rankings split.

50
00:03:07.404 --> 00:03:10.858
MoMask tops text retrieval, but its fingers jitter,

51
00:03:10.908 --> 00:03:13.880
with jerk over 18 times that of real motion.

52
00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:17.108
Kimodo is nearly as accurate, and far smoother,

53
00:03:17.158 --> 00:03:20.200
with the fewest collisions between the hands.

54
00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:20.955
The lesson:

55
00:03:21.005 --> 00:03:24.596
matching the text isn't the same as moving plausibly.

56
00:03:24.646 --> 00:03:27.000
Hand models must be judged on both.

57
00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:31.414
Because every clip carries a taxonomy label,

58
00:03:31.464 --> 00:03:34.775
we can also score models category by category.

59
00:03:34.825 --> 00:03:36.455
MDM wins communication.

60
00:03:36.505 --> 00:03:39.597
MoMask takes performance arts and assembly.

61
00:03:39.647 --> 00:03:41.715
Office depends on the metric.

62
00:03:41.765 --> 00:03:45.879
Across the 40 categories with enough test clips to score,

63
00:03:45.929 --> 00:03:48.582
MDM and MoMask split the wins evenly:

64
00:03:48.632 --> 00:03:50.920
no single model wins everywhere.

65
00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:55.437
RoMo Hands is part of the RoMo ecosystem.

66
00:03:55.487 --> 00:03:56.326
Next to it:

67
00:03:56.376 --> 00:04:00.857
RoMo Body, for full-body motion, and the Motion Toolbox,

68
00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:03.526
for evaluation and visualization.

69
00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:05.791
It all lives at romodataset.

70
00:04:05.841 --> 00:04:06.357
github.

71
00:04:06.407 --> 00:04:06.600
io.

72
00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:09.568
RoMo Hands is available now:

73
00:04:09.618 --> 00:04:14.871
the motion, the five-level captions, and the tools to evaluate and visualize it all.

74
00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:16.955
So scan the code, start building,

75
00:04:17.005 --> 00:04:20.680
and help give digital characters hands that move like ours.
